On Fri, Feb 22, 2013 at 6:27 PM, v4hn <me@v4hn.de> wrote:

> On Fri, Feb 22, 2013 at 02:52:02PM -0800, Demian Koller wrote:
> > Would someone clarify something for me regarding a sentence? It comes
> from
> > the Lojban for Beginners book, and it's an exercise in terminator
> elision: 'mi
> > skicu li re boi re lo pendo' Obviously it means 'I describe the number 2
> to
> > 2 friends.' Removing the 'boi' ('mi skicu li re re lo pendo'), I come up
> > with the meaning 'I describe the 22 individual friends.' But the book
> says
>
> That would be {mi skicu re re lo pendo}
>
> > the translation without 'boi' would be 'I describe the number 22 to a
> > friend.' Does the 'li' before 're re' imply a terminator before the 'lo'?
>
> Yes, the term governed by {li} takes up one sumti slot.
> And {li} takes the longest following string which matches a number
> expression,
> which in this case is {re re}.
>
> li is an article (gadri) just like le, lo, la, but li takes numbers after
it.

stevo

>
> v4hn
>

